= pH =

== Overview ==
'''pH''' is a logarithmic quantity used to express hydrogen-ion activity in a solution.

== Definition ==
IUPAC defines pH in terms of the activity of hydrogen(1+) ions: pH = −lg a(H+). In introductory contexts it is often approximated using hydrogen-ion concentration, but activity is the formal quantity.

== Scope & importance ==
pH is a compact way to describe acid-base conditions and strongly influences chemical equilibria, reaction rates, solubility, biological processes, corrosion, and measurement systems.

== Core concepts ==
Because the scale is logarithmic, a change of one pH unit corresponds to a tenfold change in the relevant hydrogen-ion activity. Practical pH measurement uses operational standards and electrochemical cells.

== Examples ==
Solutions with different acid-base compositions can have very different hydrogen-ion activities even when they look physically similar. Buffers resist changes in pH when small amounts of acid or base are added.

== Applications / implications ==
pH measurement is used in analytical chemistry, environmental monitoring, water treatment, biology, medicine, food science, agriculture, and industrial processing.

== Epistemic notes ==
The simple classroom formula pH = −log10[H+] is an approximation. Formal pH involves activity and practical measurement is operationally standardized; precision work should state conditions and standards.

== Acidity, activity, and concentration ==
The rigorous definition uses the dimensionless hydrogen-ion '''activity''', not simply molar concentration. In sufficiently dilute solutions, a concentration-based approximation is often adequate for introductory calculations; ionic strength and activity coefficients matter for more precise work. The conventional statement that pH 7 is neutral refers to familiar dilute aqueous conditions near room temperature, not to every solvent, temperature or concentrated mixture.

For example, if the hydrogen-ion activity rises by a factor of ten, the pH falls by one unit. The logarithmic relation is more informative than a linear “acidity scale” analogy.

== Connection to chemical equilibrium ==
[[Related to::Chemical equilibrium|Acid–base equilibria]] govern how chemical species partition between protonated and deprotonated forms. Buffer mixtures can resist changes in pH after a limited acid or base addition. Buffer capacity is finite and depends on composition; it is not an invariant property of water or of all solutions.

== Measurement and limitations ==
A glass-electrode pH reading depends on calibration, temperature, junction potentials, sample matrix and appropriate reference standards. IUPAC cautions that single-ion activities cannot be measured independently: the formal activity definition and practical pH scales therefore need to be distinguished.

